ilized a Climate Working Group composed of individuals skeptical of mainstr=
eam climate science to produce a report that informed EPA=E2=80=99s propose=
d rescission of the 2009 determination that greenhouse gases threaten the p=
ublic health and welfare of the American people (the =E2=80=9CEndangerment =
Finding=E2=80=9D). The group was formed and operated without public disclos=
ure, and its report was published concurrently with EPA=E2=80=99s proposed =
rulemaking, which cited the report extensively. On August 12, the Environme=
ntal Defense Fund and the Union of Concerned Scientists filed a<a href=3D"h=
ttps://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.mad.287748/gov.uscourts=
.mad.287748.1.0.pdf" aria-label=3D" complaint- opens in new tab" target=3D"=
_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"> complaint</a> challenging the administ=
ration=E2=80=99s conduct regarding the Climate Working Group. Plaintiffs al=
lege that the formation and use of the Climate Working Group violated proce=
dural and transparency requirements under federal law, depriving them and t=
he public of access to meetings, records, and balanced representation. They=
 claim these violations hinder their ability to participate in public comme=
nt processes and to advocate for science-based climate policy. Plaintiffs a=
llege that the administration=E2=80=99s action violates the Administrative =
Procedure Act as arbitrary, capricious, not in accordance with law, in exce=
ss of statutory jurisdiction, authority, or limitations, and without observ=
ance of procedure required by law, including the Federal Advisory Committee=
 Act, and constitutional and statutory provisions. They seek declaratory an=
d injunctive relief to vacate the establishment and actions of the Climate =
Working Group, prohibit reliance on its report, compel disclosure of relate=
d records and extend public comment periods on EPA=E2=80=99s proposed rulem=
aking.</p></td><td class=3D"column-10 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: no=
ne;"><p><strong>Sept. 17, 2025:</strong> Judge Young <a href=3D"https://sto=
