"column-3">2025-11-12</td><td class=3D"column-4"></td><td class=3D"column-5=
">Awaiting Court Ruling</td><td class=3D"column-8">2025-11-12</td><td class=
=3D"column-9 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: none;"><p>On Oct. 21, 2025,=
 Interior Secretary Doug Burgum signed an agreement to transfer protected l=
ands within the Izembek National Wildlife Refuge in exchange for lands held=
 by the King Cove Corporation (KCC), an Alaska native village corporation, =
for purposes of constructing a road connecting the communities of King Cove=
 and Cold Bay, Alaska in the Aleutian Islands (the =E2=80=98Exchange Agreem=
ent=E2=80=99). Construction of a road through the Izembek Wilderness had pr=
eviously been rejected on several occasions dating back to 1985 by prior In=
terior Secretaries, Congress, environmental assessors with the Fish and Wil=
dlife Service or Army Corps of Engineers, and federal courts for environmen=
tal and administrative deficiencies. On Nov. 12, Plaintiffs filed a suit ch=
allenging the Exchange Agreement as procedurally and substantively deficien=
t. Plaintiffs are several environmental nonprofits led by the Friends of Al=
aska National Wildlife Refuges, a volunteer organization based in Anchorage=
 that advocates for stewardship of Alaska=E2=80=99 sixteen designated Natio=
nal Wildlife Refuges. Defendants are Interior Secretary Doug Burgum in his =
official capacity, the Department of the Interior itself, the U.S. Bureau o=
f Land Management (BLM), BLM=E2=80=99s Alaska State Director Kevin Penderga=
st, the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (FWS), FWS=E2=80=99 Alaska Regional =
Director Sara Boario, and the King Cove Corporation. Plaintiffs allege the =
Exchange Agreement violates Title XI of the Alaska National Interest Lands =
Conservation Act (ANILCA), which requires that the creation of transportati=
on systems through designated conservation units follow certain procedures,=
