> in the U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia alleging that the=
 expanded facility will significantly increase the number of oil trains tra=
veling on the Union Pacific Line through Eagle County, adjacent to the Colo=
rado River and through BLM-managed public lands, raising risks of derailmen=
ts, oil spills, and wildfires. The complaint alleges that the new guidance =
is a substantive rule requiring notice-and-comment rulemaking under the Adm=
inistrative Procedure Act and Federal Land Policy and Management Act; that =
Interior=E2=80=99s invocation of emergency procedures was unlawful under th=
e APA, FLPMA, and NEPA because no qualifying =E2=80=9Csudden, urgent, unusu=
ally unexpected=E2=80=9D emergency existed; and that BLM=E2=80=99s authoriz=
ation violated NEPA and the APA by failing to assess reasonably foreseeable=
 indirect effects and arbitrarily reversing its prior commitment to public =
review and comment. The complaint also alleges BLM failed to issue a timely=
 determination on Eagle County=E2=80=99s requests under the Freedom of Info=
rmation Act. Plaintiffs seek declaratory judgments that the guidance and th=
e expansion approval are unlawful, injunctive relief vacating both, and an =
order requiring BLM to produce FOIA-responsive records.</p></td><td class=
=3D"column-10 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: none;"></td>
