d class=3D"column-9 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: none;"><p>Between Ap=
ril 2023 and January 2025, the State of California obtained three waivers f=
rom the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) which allowed California to p=
romulgate stricter emissions standards and requirements concerning zero-emi=
ssion vehicles than those imposed under, and that would otherwise be preemp=
ted by, the Clean Air Act (CAA). The waivers, which were granted under a lo=
ng-standing provision in the CAA designed by Congress to ensure that Califo=
rnia could continue to promulgate its own strict emission standards, allowe=
d California to develop regulations to strengthen the sales of zero-emissio=
n cars and trucks. On January 20, 2025, President Trump signed Executive Or=
der (EO) 14096, titled =E2=80=9CUnleashing American Energy=E2=80=9D, which =
declared that =E2=80=9Cstate emissions waivers that function to limit sales=
 of gasoline-powered automobiles=E2=80=9D should be =E2=80=9Cterminat[ed] w=
here appropriate.=E2=80=9D Consistent with this EO, in February 2025, the E=
PA announced that it would retroactively submit the three waivers to Congre=
ss as rules for consideration under the Congressional Review Act, 5 U.S.C. =
=C2=A7=C2=A7 801=E2=80=93808, (CRA) and subsequently Congress passed, and T=
rump signed, joint resolutions (the =E2=80=9CResolutions=E2=80=9D) purporti=
ng to disapprove the waivers, thereby preventing the affected states from e=
nforcing their own vehicle emissions standards.</p><p>A coalition of 11 sta=
tes who have adopted the stricter standards filed suit against the EPA, cla=
iming that the use of the CRA by Congress to invalidate the prior EPA decis=
ions granting California waivers reversed decades of consistent statutory i=
nterpretation, because the EPA=E2=80=99s waivers are adjudicatory orders, n=
ot federal rules, and thus outside the scope of the CRA. Plaintiffs allege =
that the defendants=E2=80=99 actions in invoking the CRA without any legal =
rationale or administrative process, and in opposition to determinations by=
 the Government Accountability Office and the Senate Parliamentarian, are u=
ltra vires as conduct in excess of statutory authority, contrary to law, an=
d arbitrary and capricious under the Administrative Procedure Act. The plai=
ntiffs also contend that the resolution process violated foundational const=
itutional principles, including separation of powers, the Take Care Clause,=
 and the Tenth Amendment, and seek declaratory and injunctive relief to vac=
ate the Resolutions, restore the validity of the disputed waivers, declare =
the reclassification of the waiver decisions as ultra vires, unconstitution=
al, and unlawful, and prevent enforcement of the Resolutions against their =
vehicle emission programs.</p></td><td class=3D"column-10 none dtr-hidden" =
style=3D"display: none;"><p><strong><strong>Dec. 2, 2025:</strong> </strong=
>The court <a href=3D"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.=
cand.451095/gov.uscourts.cand.451095.182.0_1.pdf" aria-label=3D"denied- ope=
