dtr-title"><strong>Case Summary</strong></span> <span class=3D"dtr-data"><p=
>Between April 2023 and January 2025, the State of California obtained thre=
e waivers from the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) which allowed Cali=
fornia to promulgate stricter emissions standards and requirements concerni=
ng zero-emission vehicles than those imposed under, and that would otherwis=
e be preempted by, the Clean Air Act (CAA). The waivers, which were granted=
 under a long-standing provision in the CAA designed by Congress to ensure =
that California could continue to promulgate its own strict emission standa=
rds, allowed California to develop regulations to strengthen the sales of z=
ero-emission cars and trucks. On January 20, 2025, President Trump signed E=
xecutive Order (EO) 14096, titled =E2=80=9CUnleashing American Energy=E2=80=
=9D, which declared that =E2=80=9Cstate emissions waivers that function to =
limit sales of gasoline-powered automobiles=E2=80=9D should be =E2=80=9Cter=
minat[ed] where appropriate.=E2=80=9D Consistent with this EO, in February =
2025, the EPA announced that it would retroactively submit the three waiver=
s to Congress as rules for consideration under the Congressional Review Act=
, 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7=C2=A7 801=E2=80=93808, (CRA) and subsequently Congress pa=
ssed, and Trump signed, joint resolutions (the =E2=80=9CResolutions=E2=80=
=9D) purporting to disapprove the waivers, thereby preventing the affected =
states from enforcing their own vehicle emissions standards.</p><p>A coalit=
ion of 11 states who have adopted the stricter standards filed suit against=
 the EPA, claiming that the use of the CRA by Congress to invalidate the pr=
ior EPA decisions granting California waivers reversed decades of consisten=
t statutory interpretation, because the EPA=E2=80=99s waivers are adjudicat=
ory orders, not federal rules, and thus outside the scope of the CRA. Plain=
tiffs allege that the defendants=E2=80=99 actions in invoking the CRA witho=
ut any legal rationale or administrative process, and in opposition to dete=
rminations by the Government Accountability Office and the Senate Parliamen=
tarian, are ultra vires as conduct in excess of statutory authority, contra=
ry to law, and arbitrary and capricious under the Administrative Procedure =
Act. The plaintiffs also contend that the resolution process violated found=
ational constitutional principles, including separation of powers, the Take=
 Care Clause, and the Tenth Amendment, and seek declaratory and injunctive =
relief to vacate the Resolutions, restore the validity of the disputed waiv=
ers, declare the reclassification of the waiver decisions as ultra vires, u=
nconstitutional, and unlawful, and prevent enforcement of the Resolutions a=
gainst their vehicle emission programs.</p></span></li><li class=3D" none" =
