>) with the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) requesting that EPA lower=
 the acceptable levels of residue for the pesticide glyphosate on oats or o=
at products. The petition presented evidence that the existing level does n=
ot satisfy the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (FFDCA) health-protecti=
ve safety standard, with particular concern for infants and young children =
who widely consume oat products as well as glyphosate=E2=80=99s potential t=
o cause cancer. EPA=E2=80=99s risk assessments identify infants and young c=
hildren as having higher dietary exposure to glyphosate relative to their b=
ody weight. Rather than issuing the decision required by statute, EPA alleg=
edly deferred resolution of the petition to a broader Federal Insecticide, =
Fungicide, and Rodenticide Act (FIFRA) registration review process it antic=
ipates completing in 2026, leaving the petition to modify the current toler=
ance for glyphosate on oats unresolved. </p><p>EWG filed a <a href=3D"https=
://www.justsecurity.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/In-Re-Environmental-Work=
ing-Group-Petition-April-21-2026.pdf">petition</a> for a writ of mandamus a=
lleging that EPA=E2=80=99s inaction constitutes agency action unlawfully wi=
thheld or unreasonably delayed under the Administrative Procedure Act, 5 U.=
S.C. =C2=A7 706(1), and that FIFRA registration review does not displace EP=
A=E2=80=99s independent FFDCA obligation to resolve a petition requesting t=
hat EPA revoke or modify the tolerance for glyphosate on oats. Petitioner s=
eeks an order compelling EPA to issue a final decision within sixty days, w=
ith the court retaining jurisdiction to ensure compliance.</p></td><td clas=
