t EPA lower the acceptable levels of residue for the pesticide glyphosate o=
n oats or oat products. The petition presented evidence that the existing l=
evel does not satisfy the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (FFDCA) heal=
th-protective safety standard, with particular concern for infants and youn=
g children who widely consume oat products as well as glyphosate=E2=80=99s =
potential to cause cancer. EPA=E2=80=99s risk assessments identify infants =
and young children as having higher dietary exposure to glyphosate relative=
 to their body weight. Rather than issuing the decision required by statute=
, EPA allegedly deferred resolution of the petition to a broader Federal In=
secticide, Fungicide, and Rodenticide Act (FIFRA) registration review proce=
ss it anticipates completing in 2026, leaving the petition to modify the cu=
rrent tolerance for glyphosate on oats unresolved. </p><p>EWG filed a <a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.justsecurity.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/In-Re-Environ=
mental-Working-Group-Petition-April-21-2026.pdf">petition</a> for a writ of=
 mandamus alleging that EPA=E2=80=99s inaction constitutes agency action un=
lawfully withheld or unreasonably delayed under the Administrative Procedur=
e Act, 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 706(1), and that FIFRA registration review does not =
displace EPA=E2=80=99s independent FFDCA obligation to resolve a petition r=
equesting that EPA revoke or modify the tolerance for glyphosate on oats. P=
etitioner seeks an order compelling EPA to issue a final decision within si=
xty days, with the court retaining jurisdiction to ensure compliance.</p></=
span></li><li class=3D" none" data-dtr-index=3D"9" data-dt-row=3D"179" data=
