d class=3D"column-9 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: none;"><p>On Feb. 6,=
 2026, President Donald Trump issued Proclamation No. 11009, =E2=80=9C<a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/02/11/2026-02812/unlea=
shing-american-commercial-fishing-in-the-atlantic" aria-label=3D"Unleashing=
 American Commercial Fishing in the Atlantic- opens in new tab" target=3D"_=
blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Unleashing American Commercial Fishing i=
n the Atlantic</a>,=E2=80=9D revoking the prohibition on commercial fishing=
 within the Northeast Canyons and Seamounts Marine National Monument =E2=80=
=94 the only marine national monument in the U.S. Atlantic. The administrat=
ion explained that =E2=80=9Cappropriately managed=E2=80=9D commercial fishi=
ng would not put the Monument=E2=80=99s objects of historic and scientific =
interest at risk. On April 6, 2026, the National Marine Fisheries Service (=
NMFS) issued a <a href=3D"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/04=
/06/2026-06663/magnuson-stevens-fishery-conservation-and-management-act-pro=
visions-modifications-to-conform-us" aria-label=3D"final rule- opens in new=
 tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">final rule</a> rescindi=
ng the regulation codifying the commercial fishing ban, effective immediate=
ly. </p><p>On May 4, 2026, Conservation Law Foundation, the Natural Resourc=
es Defense Council, the Center for Biological Diversity, and marine mammal =
consultant R. Zack Klyver filed a <a href=3D"https://www.justsecurity.org/w=
p-content/uploads/2026/05/Conservation-Law-Foundation-v.-Trump.pdf">complai=
nt</a> alleging that the administration's actions will damage ancient deep-=
sea coral ecosystems, harm endangered whales and sea turtles through entang=
lement and bycatch, and destroy the Monument=E2=80=99s value as a scientifi=
c reference site. Plaintiffs allege that the proclamation exceeds the Presi=
dent=E2=80=99s authority under the Antiquities Act, 54 U.S.C. =C2=A7 320301=
, which authorizes the creation but not the dismantling of monument protect=
ions, and violates the separation of powers by intruding on Congress's excl=
usive authority over federal property and commerce under Article IV, Sectio=
n 3 and Article I, Section 8. Plaintiffs further allege that NMFS=E2=80=99s=
 Rescission Rule violates the Administrative Procedure Act (APA), 5 U.S.C. =
=C2=A7=C2=A7 706(2)(A) and (D), and the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservati=
on and Management Act, 16 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1855(d), by relying on an unlawful =
proclamation, failing to conduct required notice-and-comment rulemaking, en=
gaging in no reasoned decision-making about what fishing restrictions would=
 protect Monument objects, and failing to prepare any environmental impact =
statement or environmental assessment under the National Environmental Poli=
cy Act (NEPA). Plaintiffs seek declarations that the proclamation is unlawf=
ul and that the Rescission Rule is arbitrary and contrary to law, vacatur o=
f the Rescission Rule, and a permanent injunction directing the agency defe=
ndants to implement the 2016 and 2021 proclamations' commercial fishing pro=
hibition.</p></td><td class=3D"column-10 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display:=
 none;"></td>
