lled into federal service, referencing 10 U.S.C. =C2=A712406.</p><p>That sa=
me day, Oregon and the City of Portland filed a<a href=3D"https://storage.c=
ourtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.ord.189270/gov.uscourts.ord.189270.1.0.=
pdf" aria-label=3D"&nbsp;complaint- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer">&nbsp;complaint</a> alleging that the statutory co=
nditions for federalizing the Guard under =C2=A712406 were not met. Plainti=
ffs note that the President=E2=80=99s authority over a state=E2=80=99s Nati=
onal Guard pursuant to =C2=A7 12406 is limited to circumstances involving a=
 foreign nation=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cinvasion,=E2=80=9D an outright =E2=80=9C=
rebellion,=E2=80=9D or where the President has been =E2=80=9Cunable with re=
gular forces=E2=80=9D to execute Federal Law through ordinary means, and ar=
gue that Defendants=E2=80=99 invocation of the prerequisites for federaliza=
tion in =C2=A7 12406 was wholly pretextual, motivated by the desire to norm=
alize the use of military troops for ordinary domestic law enforcement acti=
vity while also punishing politically disfavoured jurisdictions like Portla=
nd, Oregon.</p><p>The complaint asserts violations of the Posse Comitatus A=
ct, which forbids Defendants from employing the armed forces to engage in l=
aw enforcement =E2=80=9Cexcept in cases and under circumstances expressly a=
uthorized by the Constitution or Act of Congress=E2=80=9D ,18 U.S.C. =C2=A7=
 1385; the Tenth Amendment as disparate treatment based on a politically mo=
