ss=3D"column-8">2026-04-20</td><td class=3D"column-9 none dtr-hidden" style=
=3D"display: none;"><p>On July 24, 2025, President Donald Trump signed <a h=
ref=3D"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/DCPD-202500793/pdf/DCPD-20250079=
3.pdf" aria-label=3D"Executive Order (EO) 14321- opens in new tab" target=
=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Executive Order (EO) 14321</a>, tit=
led =E2=80=9CEnding Crime and Disorder on America=E2=80=99s Streets. The EO=
 directs the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) to stop=
 support for =E2=80=9CHousing First=E2=80=9D policies,=E2=80=9D which =E2=
=80=9Cprioritize[] rapid placement and stability in permanent housing in wh=
ich admission does not have preconditions (such as sobriety or a minimum in=
come threshold).=E2=80=9D To do this, HUD must require =E2=80=9Cpersons par=
ticipating in the recipient [states=E2=80=99] programs=E2=80=9D to take par=
t in treatment or mental health services as a precondition of obtaining hou=
sing. The EO also directs federal agencies to prioritize grants for =E2=80=
=9CStates and municipalities that actively . . . enforce prohibitions on op=
en illicit drug use; []enforce prohibitions on urban camping and loitering;=
 [and] enforce prohibitions on urban squatting.=E2=80=9D</p><p>Additionally=
, on Jan. 20, 2025, Trump signed <a href=3D"https://www.govinfo.gov/content=
/pkg/DCPD-202500135/pdf/DCPD-202500135.pdf" aria-label=3D"EO 14168- opens i=
n new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">EO 14168</a>, titl=
ed =E2=80=9CDefending Women from Gender Ideology Extremism and Restoring Bi=
ological Truth to the Federal Government.=E2=80=9D The EO directs federal a=
gencies to condition grants and contracts on compliance with prohibitions a=
gainst diversity, equity, and inclusion and =E2=80=9Cgender ideology.=E2=80=
=9D On Nov. 13, 2025, HUD issued an updated Fiscal Year 2025 Notice of Fund=
ing Opportunity (NOFO) for the Continuum of Care (CoC) Program, which is th=
e federal government=E2=80=99s primary program for funding housing for indi=
viduals at risk of homelessness. The new NOFO reversed HUD=E2=80=99s previo=
us Housing First policy, instituted new caps on certain housing projects, a=
nd conditioned grants on refraining from using the funds in a way that supp=
orts =E2=80=9Ca definition of sex as other than binary in humans.=E2=80=9D =
It also excludes substance abuse disorder from its definition of disabiliti=
es and includes a scoring system based on whether the jurisdiction is tough=
 on =E2=80=9Cvagrancy.=E2=80=9D </p><p>On Nov. 25, 2025, a coalition of 21 =
states and the District of Columbia (Plaintiff States) filed a <a href=3D"h=
ttps://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.rid.60953/gov.uscourts.=
rid.60953.1.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"complaint- opens in new tab" target=3D"_bl=
ank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">complaint</a> against HUD and Eric Scott T=
urner, in his official capacity as Secretary of HUD, alleging that the new =
conditions were unlawfully adopted without meaningful public input, impose =
impermissible conditions on the grant of funding, and are discriminatory. T=
he Plaintiff States claim that this was an <em>ultra vires </em>agency acti=
on and violated the separation of powers. The Plaintiff States further alle=
ge that the conditions violate the Administrative Procedure Act (APA) as th=
ey are contrary to law, arbitrary and capricious, contrary to constitutiona=
l right, and were enacted without observance of procedure required by law. =
Plaintiff States also allege Defendants acted in violation of the Spending =
Clause of the U.S. Constitution and the Tenth Amendment. Plaintiffs seek de=
claratory and injunctive relief.</p></td><td class=3D"column-10 none dtr-hi=
