ting regulations, including 8 C.F.R. =C2=A7 1003.47(d), which makes DHS res=
ponsible for obtaining biometrics from any detained noncitizen; was made wi=
thout a reasoned explanation and therefore arbitrary and capricious in viol=
ation of the APA, 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 706(2)(A); agency action unlawfully withh=
eld under 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 706(1); promulgated without notice-and-comment ru=
lemaking or 30-day advance publication in violation of 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 553;=
 and a violation of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause. Plaintiffs seek=
 class certification, a declaratory judgment, a stay of the policy under 5 =
U.S.C. =C2=A7 705, vacatur of the policy under 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 706, and pre=
liminary and permanent injunctive relief compelling DHS to collect class me=
mbers' biometrics.</p></td><td class=3D"column-10 none dtr-hidden" style=3D=
