=3D"complaint- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferr=
er">complaint</a> on July 25, 2025, citing President Donald Trump=E2=80=99s=
 campaign promise to terminate Haiti=E2=80=99s TPS among other public state=
ments as evidence of the absence of the statutorily designated mandated rev=
iew process, which requires the Secretary of DHS to review a country=E2=80=
=99s TPS designation based on evidence-driven review of country conditions =
and timely notice of DHS=E2=80=99s findings. According to the complaint, th=
e proffered justification that Haitian TPS holders are national security ri=
sks is pretextual, and Trump=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cracial, ethnic, and nationa=
l-origin animus=E2=80=9D is the root of the termination. As a result of the=
 status termination, Plaintiff TPS holders will lose thor ability to legall=
y work in the United States and could face imminent danger and possible dea=
th if forced to return to Haiti. The complaint alleges that the termination=
 violates the Administrative Procedure Act (APA) for being arbitrary, capri=
cious, and an abuse of discretion. The complaint further alleges the termin=
ation is in violation of the Equal Protection Clause of the Fifth Amendment=
.  Plaintiffs seek an order to declare the termination unlawful and to set =
it aside. </p></span></li><li class=3D" none" data-dtr-index=3D"9" data-dt-=
