=3D"column-8"><span style=3D"color:#111111">2026-04-24</span></td><td class=
=3D"column-9 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: none;"><p>On Jan. 20, 2025,=
 President Trump issued Proclamation 10888, =E2=80=9CGuaranteeing the State=
s Protection Against Invasion,=E2=80=9D declaring that there is an =E2=80=
=9Cinvasion=E2=80=9D at the southern border (<a href=3D"https://www.federal=
register.gov/documents/2025/01/29/2025-01951/guaranteeing-the-states-protec=
tion-against-invasion" aria-label=3D"Proclamation 10888- opens in new tab" =
target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Proclamation 10888</a>). Proc=
lamation 10888 precludes noncitizens =E2=80=9Cengaged in the invasion=E2=80=
=9D from invoking INA provisions that would permit their continued presence=
 in the United States=E2=80=94including, but not limited to, the right to s=
eek asylum under 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1158=E2=80=94and directs the Secretary of =
Homeland Security to =E2=80=9Ctake all appropriate action to repel, repatri=
ate, or remove=E2=80=9D those noncitizens. In practice, it prevents anyone =
who crosses the southern border between ports of entry, or who enters anywh=
ere else (even at a port of entry) without a visa and extensive background =
documentation, from invoking the usual INA provisions that would allow them=
 to remain in the U.S. while pursuing asylum or related protections.. Plain=
tiffs comprise thirteen individuals who are or were subject to Proclamation=
 10888 and three nonprofit organizations in Texas and Arizona providing leg=
al services and assistance to undocumented individuals or asylum seekers. I=
n a suit filed on Feb. 3, Plaintiffs argue that the order violates the foll=
owing statutory and constitutional provisions:</p><ol> <li> <p>the Asylum S=
tatute in the INA, 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1158(a)(1) (by barring noncitizens from =
applying for asylum in direct contradiction to congressional protections);<=
/p> </li> <li> <p>the Withholding of Removal Statute, 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1231(=
b)(3) (by preventing noncitizens from seeking protection from persecution b=
ased on race, religion, nationality, membership in a particular social grou=
p, or political opinion);</p> </li> <li> <p>the Foreign Affairs Reform and =
Restructuring Act (FARRA), 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1231, and the Convention Against=
 Torture (CAT) (by depriving noncitizens of a meaningful opportunity to pre=
sent CAT claims and shielding them from potential torture);</p> </li> <li> =
<p>the Trafficking Victims Protection Reauthorization Act (TVPRA), 8 U.S.C.=
 =C2=A7 1232(a)(5)(D) (by denying unaccompanied children from non-contiguou=
s countries their statutory right to regular removal proceedings);</p> </li=
> <li> <p>the INA=E2=80=99s procedural protections for removal, 8 U.S.C. =
=C2=A7=C2=A7 1101, 1229a, 1225(b) (by overriding mandated removal proceedin=
gs and eliminating procedural protections, including credible fear screenin=
gs);</p> </li> <li> <p>the Administrative Procedure Act (APA), 5 U.S.C. =C2=
=A7 706(2)(A) (by implementing policies that are arbitrary, capricious, and=
 contrary to law) and =C2=A7 706(2)(D) (by failing to follow the required r=
ulemaking process before enacting sweeping changes to statutory protections=
); and</p> </li></ol><p>the constitutional separation of powers (by exceedi=
ng presidential authority and unlawfully overriding congressionally enacted=
 immigration protections).<br>The Plaintiffs seek a declaratory judgment th=
at the proclamation is unlawful and an injunction stopping its implementati=
on.</p></td><td class=3D"column-10 none dtr-hidden" style=3D"display: none;=
"><p><strong><strong>Feb. 19, 2025:</strong></strong> Plaintiffs filed an <=
