dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039.50.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"&nbsp;amicus bri=
ef- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">&nbsp;a=
micus brief</a> in support of Defendants.</p><p><strong><strong>April 7, 20=
25:</strong></strong> Plaintiffs filed a formal <a href=3D"https://storage.=
courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039.51.=
0.pdf" aria-label=3D"motion- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noo=
pener noreferrer">motion</a> for summary judgment (and <a href=3D"https://s=
torage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277=
039.52.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"memorandum- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank"=
 rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">memorandum</a> in support), following the cour=
t=E2=80=99s decision to convert their Feb. 19 preliminary-injunction motion=
 into a summary-judgment motion. They also submitted a <a href=3D"https://s=
torage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277=
039.51.1.pdf" aria-label=3D"proposed order- opens in new tab" target=3D"_bl=
ank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">proposed order</a> requesting a permanent =
injunction to prevent the Trump administration from enforcing Proclamation =
10888</p><p><strong>Late March 2025 through early May 2025:</strong> The pa=
rties briefed Plaintiffs=E2=80=99 motion for class certification and their =
cross-motions for summary judgment.</p><p><strong><strong>July 2, 2025:</st=
rong></strong> The court <a href=3D"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap=
/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039.73.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"gr=
anted- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">gran=
ted</a> in part and deferred in part Plaintiffs=E2=80=99 motion to certify =
a class, <a href=3D"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dc=
d.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039.72.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"certifying- opens =
in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">certifying</a> un=
der Rule 23(b)(2) a class of individuals who are or will be subject to Proc=
lamation 10888 while present in the United States and designating Individua=
l Plaintiffs as class representatives. Further, in an <a href=3D"https://st=
orage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.2770=
39.73.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"order- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer">order</a> and <a href=3D"https://storage.courtlist=
ener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039.71.0.pdf" ar=
ia-label=3D"opinion- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener no=
referrer">opinion</a> the court granted in part, denied in part, and deferr=
ed in part Plaintiffs=E2=80=99 motion for summary judgment, and deferred =
=E2=80=9Cruling on the remaining portions of the parties=E2=80=99 cross-mot=
ions.=E2=80=9D In granting partial summary judgment to the certified class =
and individual Plaintiffs, the court vacated the agency guidance implementi=
ng Proclamation 10888 to the extent it authorizes "extra-statutory" or "ext=
ra-regulatory" removals or repatriations, bars class members from applying =
for asylum or, where eligible, withholding of removal, or departs from the =
existing CAT-screening regulations. The court declared Proclamation 10888 u=
nlawful insofar as it purports to suspend or restrict access to asylum, wit=
hholding of removal, or CAT procedures, or to authorize extra-statutory rem=
oval mechanisms, and permanently enjoined the agency defendants from using =
non-statutory repatriation procedures or removing class members without com=
plying with the asylum statute and governing CAT regulations. On that same =
day, the government <a href=3D"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.=
uscourts.dcd.277039/gov.uscourts.dcd.277039.74.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"appeale=
d- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">appealed=
</a> Judge Moss=E2=80=99s order granting summary judgment to the D.C. Circu=
it.</p><p><strong><strong>July 11, 2025:</strong></strong> The D.C. Circuit=
 granted an <a href=3D"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts=
.cadc.42189/gov.uscourts.cadc.42189.01208756530.0_1.pdf" aria-label=3D"admi=
nistrative stay- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener norefe=
rrer">administrative stay</a> of Judge Moss=E2=80=99s July 2 summary judgme=
nt.</p><p><strong><strong>Aug. 1, 2025:</strong></strong> The D.C. Circuit =
dissolved its July 11 administrative stay and <a href=3D"https://storage.co=
urtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cadc.42189/gov.uscourts.cadc.42189.01208=
763191.1_1.pdf" aria-label=3D"granted- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" =
rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">granted</a> in part and denied in part the gove=
rnment=E2=80=99s emergency motion for a stay pending appeal of Judge Moss=
=E2=80=99s July 2 summary judgment. The court clarified that the class cons=
ists only of individuals who are present in the United States while Proclam=
ation 10888 or its implementation is in effect, are not statutorily ineligi=
ble for asylum, statutory withholding, or CAT protection, and who would oth=
erwise seek one of those forms of relief. It also stayed the portions of th=
e district court=E2=80=99s judgment that (1) vacated the Proclamation=E2=80=
=99s implementing guidance on asylum applications, (2) enjoined removal of =
class members without complying with the asylum statute, and (3) declared t=
he Proclamation unlawful insofar as it suspends or restricts access to asyl=
um, denying a stay in all other respects.</p><p><strong><strong>April 24, 2=
026:</strong> </strong>The D.C. Circuit Court <a href=3D"https://storage.co=
urtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cadc.42189/gov.uscourts.cadc.42189.01208=
844046.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"affirmed- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" r=
el=3D"noopener noreferrer">affirmed</a> the district court=E2=80=99s grant =
of summary judgment and class certification, holding in an accompanying <a =
href=3D"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cadc.42189/gov=
.uscourts.cadc.42189.1208844052.0.pdf" aria-label=3D"opinion- opens in new =
tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">opinion</a> that the Pro=
clamation and Guidance are unlawful to the extent they circumvent the INA=
=E2=80=99s removal procedures and strip protections for asylum and withhold=
ing of removal. Judge Childs, writing for the court, wrote that the INA did=
 not provide the =E2=80=9Cexpansive removal authority=E2=80=9D asserted in =
<a href=3D"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/proclamation-10888-gua=
ranteeing-the-states-protection-against-invasion" aria-label=3D"Proclamatio=
n 10888- opens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Pr=
oclamation 10888</a>. In a partial concurrence, Judge Walker agreed that th=
e INA did not provide sufficient authority but dissented regarding class ce=
