dtr-title"><strong>Case Summary</strong></span> <span class=3D"dtr-data"><p=
>On Jan. 20, 2025, President Trump issued Proclamation 10888, =E2=80=9CGuar=
anteeing the States Protection Against Invasion,=E2=80=9D declaring that th=
ere is an =E2=80=9Cinvasion=E2=80=9D at the southern border (<a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/01/29/2025-01951/guaranteeing-t=
he-states-protection-against-invasion" aria-label=3D"Proclamation 10888- op=
ens in new tab" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Proclamation =
10888</a>). Proclamation 10888 precludes noncitizens =E2=80=9Cengaged in th=
e invasion=E2=80=9D from invoking INA provisions that would permit their co=
ntinued presence in the United States=E2=80=94including, but not limited to=
, the right to seek asylum under 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1158=E2=80=94and directs t=
he Secretary of Homeland Security to =E2=80=9Ctake all appropriate action t=
o repel, repatriate, or remove=E2=80=9D those noncitizens. In practice, it =
prevents anyone who crosses the southern border between ports of entry, or =
who enters anywhere else (even at a port of entry) without a visa and exten=
sive background documentation, from invoking the usual INA provisions that =
would allow them to remain in the U.S. while pursuing asylum or related pro=
tections.. Plaintiffs comprise thirteen individuals who are or were subject=
 to Proclamation 10888 and three nonprofit organizations in Texas and Arizo=
na providing legal services and assistance to undocumented individuals or a=
sylum seekers. In a suit filed on Feb. 3, Plaintiffs argue that the order v=
iolates the following statutory and constitutional provisions:</p><ol> <li>=
 <p>the Asylum Statute in the INA, 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1158(a)(1) (by barring n=
oncitizens from applying for asylum in direct contradiction to congressiona=
l protections);</p> </li> <li> <p>the Withholding of Removal Statute, 8 U.S=
.C. =C2=A7 1231(b)(3) (by preventing noncitizens from seeking protection fr=
om persecution based on race, religion, nationality, membership in a partic=
ular social group, or political opinion);</p> </li> <li> <p>the Foreign Aff=
airs Reform and Restructuring Act (FARRA), 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1231, and the Co=
nvention Against Torture (CAT) (by depriving noncitizens of a meaningful op=
portunity to present CAT claims and shielding them from potential torture);=
</p> </li> <li> <p>the Trafficking Victims Protection Reauthorization Act (=
TVPRA), 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1232(a)(5)(D) (by denying unaccompanied children fr=
om non-contiguous countries their statutory right to regular removal procee=
dings);</p> </li> <li> <p>the INA=E2=80=99s procedural protections for remo=
val, 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7=C2=A7 1101, 1229a, 1225(b) (by overriding mandated rem=
oval proceedings and eliminating procedural protections, including credible=
 fear screenings);</p> </li> <li> <p>the Administrative Procedure Act (APA)=
, 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 706(2)(A) (by implementing policies that are arbitrary, c=
apricious, and contrary to law) and =C2=A7 706(2)(D) (by failing to follow =
the required rulemaking process before enacting sweeping changes to statuto=
ry protections); and</p> </li></ol><p>the constitutional separation of powe=
rs (by exceeding presidential authority and unlawfully overriding congressi=
onally enacted immigration protections).<br>The Plaintiffs seek a declarato=
ry judgment that the proclamation is unlawful and an injunction stopping it=
s implementation.</p></span></li><li class=3D" none" data-dtr-index=3D"9" d=
ata-dt-row=3D"484" data-dt-column=3D"9"><span class=3D"dtr-title"><strong>C=
