habeas relief to Avila, holding that he was properly subject to mandatory d=
etention under 8 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1225(b)(2) and, therefore, not entitled to a=
 bond hearing. The court concluded that even non-citizens long present in t=
he U.S. can be treated as =E2=80=9Capplicants for admission,=E2=80=9D dismi=
ssing the district court=E2=80=99s narrower interpretation and remanding it=
 for further proceedings. The dissent argued that the majority=E2=80=99s re=
ading departed from longstanding practice and improperly expanded mandatory=
 detention. </p></td>
