 alleging that their mass terminations were a pretextual, politically motiv=
ated and unconstitutional reduction-in-force (RIF). Plaintiffs claim that d=
espite their formal appointment to federal service as employees, legally di=
stinguishing them from contractors, USAID treated plaintiffs like contracto=
rs who may be terminated at will and without prior notice or any post-termi=
nation recourse to administrative or judicial review. The complaint alleges=
 that the termination was punishment for their perceived ideological affili=
ation and partisan beliefs, loosely disguised as a RIF.</p><p>Plaintiffs as=
sert that their terminations violate their constitutional rights including =
the First Amendment protection against removal based on political affiliati=
on, and Fifth Amendment Due Process protection in USAID following its adopt=
ed regulations. Plaintiffs also allege violations of the Administrative Lea=
ve Act (ALA), which prevents defendants from placing an employee on adminis=
trative leave for more than a total of ten workdays, and the Administrative=
 Procedure Act (APA), contending that the termination was arbitrary and cap=
ricious because it was pretextual, detached from any review of Plaintiffs=
=E2=80=99 individual performance, and without any rational connection betwe=
en the grounds charged and the interest assertedly served by terminating th=
ese employees; contrary to constitutional rights because it violated their =
First Amendment free speech and association rights as civil servants; and i=
n excess of statutory authority because it violated their rights under the =
ALA and the Civil Service Reform Act (CSRA) against partisan removal. They =
also allege that the terminations represent a <em>mala fide</em> RIF, as th=
ey were removed =E2=80=9Con the basis of misconduct,=E2=80=9D disguised as =
a RIF, but denied their right to a hearing and review under the Foreign Ser=
vice Act, 22 U.S.C. =C2=A7 4010. They seek a declaratory judgment that Plai=
ntiffs possess the same rights as career Foreign Service Officers also hire=
d through appointment and not contract and that the terminations were unlaw=
ful and unconstitutional, ask the court to enjoin both the terminations and=
 the use of evaluation of partisan affiliation of future applicants for Pla=
intiffs=E2=80=99 positions, and request an order requiring the defendants t=
o individually evaluate each plaintiff=E2=80=99s performance during their t=
enure, declare the reasons for terminations, correct their personnel files,=
 and give them an opportunity to be restored to similar positions.</p></td>=
