ce with protections under the U.S. Constitution, American Oversight, a nonp=
artisan, non-profit organization engaged in disseminating information about=
 the government to the public, allegedly submitted three Freedom of Informa=
tion Act (FOIA), 5 U.S.C. =C2=A7 552, requests to CBP on Jan. 26, 2026. Spe=
cifically, American Oversight sought records related to CBP=E2=80=99s expan=
ded operations and use of force=E2=80=93including training materials, the c=
ommunications of former CBP Chief Gregory Bovino, and legal advice and anal=
ysis concerning the Fourth Amendment and stop, search, and seizure authorit=
y. CBP allegedly acknowledged receipt of these requests, but has otherwise =
failed to respond. On March 4, 2026, American Oversight <a href=3D"https://=
www.justsecurity.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Oversight-v.-Customs-and-Bo=
rder-Protection-Complaint.pdf">sued</a> CBP, alleging it violated FOIA by f=
ailing to conduct adequate searches for responsive records, wrongfully with=
holding non-exempt agency records, and failing to provide all non-exempt re=
sponsive records. Plaintiff seeks that court to compel CBP=E2=80=99s compli=
ance with the requirements of FOIA by releasing the requested records and p=
rohibiting CBP from continuing to withhold records responsive to American O=
versight=E2=80=99s FOIA requests.</p></span></li><li class=3D" none" data-d=
